.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt SYSLOG.CONF 5
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm syslog.conf
.Nd configuration file for syslogd
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
file tells
.Xr syslogd 8
where to write each message it receives.
Each line is a rule: a
.Em selector ,
white space, and an
.Em action .
A
.Ql #
starts a comment line, and blank lines are ignored.
Every rule whose selector takes a message is carried out, in the order of
the file.
A line that cannot be parsed is reported and left out; the rest of the file
still applies.
.Ss Selectors
A selector is one or more
.Ar facility . Ns Ar level
terms joined by
.Ql \&; .
Several facilities may share one level, joined by
.Ql \&, .
A later term replaces what earlier ones said about the facilities it names,
so
.Ql *.notice;mail.none
selects
.Cm notice
and above from every facility but
.Cm mail .
.Pp
The facilities are
.Cm auth ,
.Cm authpriv ,
.Cm console ,
.Cm cron ,
.Cm daemon ,
.Cm ftp ,
.Cm kern ,
.Cm lpr ,
.Cm mail ,
.Cm mark ,
.Cm news ,
.Cm ntp ,
.Cm security ,
.Cm syslog ,
.Cm user ,
.Cm uucp
and
.Cm local0
to
.Cm local7 .
.Ql *
is every facility but
.Cm mark ,
which must be named.
.Pp
The levels, most severe first, are
.Cm emerg
.Pq or Cm panic ,
.Cm alert ,
.Cm crit ,
.Cm err
.Pq or Cm error ,
.Cm warning
.Pq or Cm warn ,
.Cm notice ,
.Cm info
and
.Cm debug .
A level selects that level and every more severe one.
It may be preceded by a comparison:
.Ql =
selects only that level,
.Ql >
the more severe levels,
.Ql <
the less severe ones, and
.Ql >= ,
.Ql <=
and
.Ql <>
as expected.
A
.Ql \&!
before it selects the levels it would not.
The level
.Ql *
selects every level, and
.Cm none
none, excluding the facility.
Names are not case-sensitive.
.Ss Actions
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa /path
Append to the file, which must exist
.Po see
.Xr syslogd 8 Ns 's
.Fl C
.Pc ,
writing it to disk after each line.
A path that names a terminal, such as
.Pa /dev/console ,
is written as a terminal, each line ending in a carriage return and a
newline.
.It Li - Ns Pa /path
The same, without writing to disk after each line.
.It Li \&| Ns Ar command
Write each line to the standard input of
.Ql /bin/sh -c Ar command ,
started with the first message and again after it exits.
Its standard output and error go to
.Pa /dev/null .
.It Li @ Ns Ar host Ns Op : Ns Ar port
Send to
.Xr syslogd 8
on another host, over UDP, port 514 by default.
.It Li @@ Ns Ar host Ns Op : Ns Ar port
Send over TCP, port 514 by default, each message preceded by its length
(RFC 6587 octet counting).
.It Li @[ Ns Ar host Ns Li \&] Ns Oo : Ns Ar port Oc Ns Op Li \&( Ns Ar options Ns Li \&)
Send over TLS, port 6514 by default, framed as over TCP, in RFC 5424 form.
The server's certificate must be vouched for by a trusted authority
.Pq Va tls_ca , Va tls_cadir , No or else the system's, see Xr certctl 8
and name
.Ar host ,
unless
.Ar options ,
a comma-separated list of
.Ar name Ns Li = Ns Qq Ar value ,
say otherwise:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Li subject= Ns Qq Ar name
The certificate's common name or a
.Li subjectAltName
must be
.Ar name
instead of
.Ar host .
.It Li fingerprint= Ns Qq Li SHA-256: Ns Ar hex
Accept exactly the certificate whose SHA-256 fingerprint this is, whoever
vouches for it.
.It Li cert= Ns Ar file
Accept exactly the certificate in
.Ar file .
.It Li verify= Ns Qq Li off
Accept any certificate.
.El
.Pp
While a TCP or TLS connection is down its messages are queued, up to 1024 or
1 MiB, the oldest dropped first and the number dropped logged, and
.Xr syslogd 8
connects again after 10 seconds, the delay doubling up to 10 minutes.
.It Ar user Ns Op , Ns Ar user ...
Write to every terminal where one of these users is logged in.
.It Li *
Write to every logged-in user.
.El
.Ss Blocks
These lines apply to the rules that follow them, until the same kind of
line changes them:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Li \&! Ns Ar prog Ns Op , Ns Ar prog ...
Only messages whose tag is one of these programs.
.It Li !- Ns Ar prog Ns Op , Ns Ar prog ...
Only messages whose tag is none of these.
.It Li + Ns Ar host Ns Op , Ns Ar host ...
Only messages from these hosts;
.Ql @
is this host.
.It Li - Ns Ar host Ns Op , Ns Ar host ...
Only messages from any host but these.
.It Li \&: Ns Ar property , Oo Li \&! Oc Ns Oo Li icase_ Oc Ns Ar operator , Li \(dq Ns Ar value Ns Li \(dq
Only messages whose
.Ar property ,
one of
.Cm msg
(the text),
.Cm programname
(the tag),
.Cm hostname
and
.Cm source ,
passes the comparison.
The operators are
.Cm contains ,
.Cm isequal ,
.Cm startswith ,
.Cm regex
(a basic regular expression) and
.Cm ereg
(an extended one, see
.Xr re_format 7 ) .
.Li icase_
ignores case,
.Li \&!
negates.
Inside the quotes,
.Ql \e
quotes the next character.
.El
.Pp
.Ql \&!* ,
.Ql +*
and
.Ql :*
end the block of their kind.
.Ql #!
and
.Ql #+
are older spellings of
.Ql \&!
and
.Ql + .
.Ss Other lines
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Li include Ar path
Read another file, or every file ending in
.Pa .conf
in a directory, in name order.
A path that does not exist is ignored.
.It Ar name Ns Li = Ns Ar value
A global option, in
.Nx Ns 's
form.
The options are those of TCP and TLS; another is reported as unknown.
A boolean is
.Li on
or
.Li off .
.Bl -tag -width tls_allow_fingerprints
.It Va tcp_server
Receive over TCP, octet-counted or newline-terminated messages.
Default off.
.It Va tcp_bindhost , Va tcp_bindport
The address and port to listen on: all addresses and 514 by default.
.It Va tls_server
Receive over TLS.
Default off; needs
.Va tls_keyfile
and
.Va tls_certfile .
.It Va tls_bindhost , Va tls_bindport
All addresses and 6514 by default.
.It Va tls_keyfile , Va tls_certfile
This host's private key and certificate, in PEM form: presented as a server,
and as a client when sending over TLS.
.It Va tls_gen_cert
Make a key and a self-signed certificate at those paths if either is
missing, logging its fingerprint.
Default off.
.It Va tls_ca , Va tls_cadir
A file of trusted authorities, and a directory of them as
.Xr certctl 8
lays one out.
With neither, the system's
.Pq Pa /etc/ssl/cert.pem , Pa /etc/ssl/certs .
.It Va tls_verify
Whether a client must present a certificate a trusted authority vouches for,
or one of those allowed below.
Default on.
.It Va tls_allow_fingerprints
Comma-separated
.Li SHA-256: Ns Ar hex
fingerprints of client certificates accepted besides.
.It Va tls_allow_clientcerts
Comma-separated files of client certificates accepted besides.
.El
.Pp
TLS 1.2 is the least version used.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/etc/syslog.d -compact
.It Pa /etc/syslog.conf
.It Pa /etc/syslog.d
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/syslog.d
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default
.Pa /etc/syslog.conf :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
*.err;kern.warning;auth.notice;mail.crit		/dev/console
*.notice;authpriv.none;kern.debug;lpr.info;mail.crit;news.err	/var/log/messages
security.*					/var/log/security
auth.info;authpriv.info				/var/log/auth.log
mail.info					/var/log/maillog
cron.*						/var/log/cron
*.emerg						*
include						/etc/syslog.d
include						/usr/local/etc/syslog.d
.Ed
.Pp
Everything from
.Xr sshd 8 ,
and everything else except debugging messages to another host:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
!sshd
*.*			/var/log/sshd.log
!*
*.info			@loghost
.Ed
.Pp
Messages from this host mentioning a failure, in any case, to a script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
+@
:msg, icase_contains, "fail"
*.*			|/usr/local/sbin/alert
:*
+*
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr logger 1 ,
.Xr syslog 3 ,
.Xr newsyslog.conf 5 ,
.Xr certctl 8 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
file first appeared in
.Bx 4.3 .
Its format here is
.Fx Ns 's ,
with
.Nx Ns 's
global options.
.Sh BUGS
Unlike
.Fx ,
.Ql #-
does not start a host block, so a comment of dashes stays a comment.
